<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philippine Welser</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1527">1527</a>–<a href="/years/1580">1580</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philippine Welser was the daughter of a merchant of the wealthy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/welser-family">Welser family</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>. In 1557 she secretly married Archduke Ferdinand II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, a marriage later accepted on condition that their children could not inherit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands. She lived at Ambras Castle near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a> and is remembered for her cookbook and medical recipes; her son Charles became Margrave of Burgau.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philippine-welser.jpg" alt="Philippine Welser" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Philippine_Welser_(1527-1580).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Philippine's father was Franz (Friedrich) Welser, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> merchant and patrician, and her mother Anna Adler von Zinnenburg, a sister of the Czech aristocrat Catherine Lokšanská de Lokšany. She was a niece of the merchant and banker Bartholomeus V. Welser, though the sources leave the relationship uncertain. So little is known of her youth that myths grew up soon after her death, the oldest and most persistent being that of her exceptional beauty. The first documented contact with Ferdinand, then governor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, is a masked ball at the carnival in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plzen">Plzeň</a> in February 1555.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Her aunt Catharina of Lokšany became Ferdinand's confidante and may have brought the couple together at Březnice Castle, which became Philippine's residence and the scene of the secret wedding, dated by Ferdinand's own assurance of 1576. By 1559 at the latest Emperor Ferdinand I knew of the marriage and agreed a settlement: it was to stay secret, and the children, excluded from the succession, were to receive lordships, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> arms and financial support. Their children were officially taken in at the castle as foundlings. The secrecy ended in 1576, when their eldest son Andrew was to become a cardinal and needed proof of legitimate birth.</p><p>At Ambras Castle, rebuilt as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> residence, she laid out a herb garden and mixed medicines with her physician Dr Georg Handsch and her pharmacist. A pharmacopoeia sometimes attributed to her was written by her mother, and the cookbook long credited to her was most likely commissioned by Anna and written by at least three scribes, though Philippine made or ordered additions. Many petitions to her from commoners and nobles in need survive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>From 1570 she suffered considerable ill health, and she died on 24 April 1580. Ferdinand gave her a white marble tomb in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> chapel of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a> court church and provided for her servants and for the poor she had supported for the rest of his life. Of her four children the twins Maria and Philipp died as infants; their coffins, secretly buried at Innsbruck, were found during restoration work in 1897. Andrew became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Constance and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brixen">Brixen</a>, and Charles an imperial general in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philippine Welser” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1609 Günzburg was the residence of Margrave Charles of Burgau, son of Archduke Ferdinand II and <b>Philippine Welser</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-burgau">Margraviate of Burgau</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Ambras Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.37,10.9&amp;year=1561" aria-label="Augsburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Philippine Welser"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5615 5937h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5615 5937h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.37,10.9&amp;year=1561">What was here in 1561? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
